{
  "correction_rules": [
    "Persistent positive bias: model forecasts are systematically too high versus realized prices (often by ~5–15 EUR). Apply a downward level shift to the whole path; larger shift when recent history is falling.",
    "Trend/turning-point lag: in selloffs the model underestimates downside speed and keeps prices elevated for ~1–2 weeks. Add a momentum correction: if the last 5–10 days trend is negative, reduce near-term (days 1–10) forecasts more aggressively than later horizons.",
    "Insufficient mean-reversion to downside: after a high-price regime (80s) the model projects stabilization/rebound, but outcomes continue reverting lower (high 60s/low 70s). Enforce stronger mean reversion toward a lower short-term anchor when the last price is below the 10–20d average.",
    "Horizon-shaped error: overprediction grows with horizon (mid/late path too high). Use a horizon-dependent haircut (small for days 1–5, larger for days 10–30).",
    "Overshoot after spikes: when history contains a recent peak, the model extrapolates elevated levels; realized path fades. If max(last 10–20d) is far above the latest close, cap rebounds and bias the forecast toward continued drift down rather than recovery."
  ]
}
